      "claim": "Human amitriptyline oral bioavailability values of 40-60% (C04), 46±48% (C07), 48±11% (C12), 47.7±11.0% (C19), and 30-60% (C26) show that a lipophilic tricyclic tertiary amine with a basic side chain can exceed the 20% F threshold; the rat value of 4.4% (C29) is species-specific and does not override the consistent human data.",
      "card_ids": ["C04", "C07", "C12", "C19", "C26", "C29"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Human alfentanil oral bioavailability values of 37% (C01), 0.45±0.09 (C11), 0.42±0.15 (C18), and 41% (C28) support moderate oral bioavailability for the query's anilino-basic-amine scaffold; the single 1% table value (C13) is an outlier within that context.",
      "card_ids": ["C01", "C11", "C18", "C28", "C13"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 records show that structurally similar lipophilic basic amines are well absorbed in humans: imipramine human Fa is essentially 100% (C43, C48, C49, C53), amitriptyline HCl is freely soluble in water (C45), orphenadrine has a human FA of 95% (C52), and chloroquine is highly absorbed despite being a dicationic basic drug (C44). This directly supports the absorption component of F>=20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C43", "C44", "C45", "C46", "C48", "C49", "C52", "C53"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Low-F records for selegiline (C02, C03, C06, C08, C09, C14, C16, C23), rotigotine/N-0437 (C10, C17, C25, C27), and brexanolone (C20) depend on specific liabilities—propargyl/MAO metabolism, phenolic conjugation/GI mucosal metabolism, and steroid scaffold—that are absent in the query, so they do not affirmatively establish F<20% for the query.",
      "card_ids": ["C02", "C03", "C06", "C08", "C09", "C14", "C16", "C23", "C10", "C17", "C25", "C27", "C20"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L3 oral AUC records for imipramine and desipramine (C31, C34, C35, C42) show measurable systemic exposure after oral dosing in similar lipophilic basic amines, consistent with meaningful oral absorption; they do not provide absolute F but do not contradict the high prediction.",
      "card_ids": ["C31", "C34", "C35", "C42"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "intestinal_absorption_permeability",
      "applicability": "high",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C43", "C44", "C45", "C46", "C48", "C49", "C52", "C53"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct oral bioavailability measurement exists for the query molecule; the decision relies on transfer from analog contexts.",
    "No query-specific first-pass metabolism, hepatic/intestinal clearance, or efflux data are available; L5 gut-wall efflux and intestinal-metabolism evidence is not yet provided.",
    "L4 absorption records are for analogs (imipramine, amitriptyline, orphenadrine, chloroquine), not the query itself.",
    "The query is fully ionized at physiological pH (pKa 10.02, two basic sites); although L4 shows similar ionized basic amines are well absorbed, query-specific permeability is not measured.",
    "The alfentanil context contains a conflicting 1% F record (C13), adding uncertainty to that analog.",
    "L3 AUC/Cmax records lack IV comparators, so they cannot independently establish absolute F."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Predicted high (F>=20%) because the query's lipophilic basic diamine/aniline scaffold is best matched by human amitriptyline (F ~30-60%) and alfentanil (F ~37-45%), and L4 records show essentially complete human intestinal absorption for similar basic amines (imipramine Fa ~100%, orphenadrine FA 95%, chloroquine highly absorbed). Low-F analogs carry specific metabolic liabilities absent in the query. Confidence remains moderate because all evidence is transferred and first-pass/efflux data are not yet available."
}

